package guild

import (
	db "{{MODULE}}/db"
	"github.com/tjbdwanghaibo/roost-core/entity"
)

const EntityKindGuild entity.EntityKind = 5

// Guild is the demo's first entity that does NOT belong to one process.
//
// Every other entity here is owned by the process that loaded it: a Player is
// on the process its connection landed on, a Monster is on the process that
// spawned it, the World is a per-process singleton. A guild cannot be any of
// those, because the players in it are on different processes and they all
// have to change the same roster.
//
// `remote=managed` is how that is expressed. The kind's ids carry the
// remote-capable bit, so Nest recognises a transaction that touches one and
// prepares it through the remote entity manager: a versioned distributed lock
// (Redis), a load from the authoritative store, the handler under that lock,
// and a fenced commit. The process that happens to hold the lock is the owner
// FOR THAT TRANSACTION and for no longer than that.
//
// What it costs, said plainly: every transaction on a guild is a distributed
// lock round trip. That is why the demo puts the roster here and leaves
// everything a single player does — the bag, the position, the equipment — on
// the Player, where it is a local lock.
//
// Category: EntityCategoryRemote, which is rank 1 — before everything. That is
// not a choice: a remote-managed kind MUST be in it, and the framework's
// registry validation refuses anything else. The reason is worth keeping in
// mind while reading the JoinGuild handler: the distributed lock is taken at
// the top of the dispatch, and taking it while a local entity mutex was
// already held would hold that mutex across a network round trip — every other
// user of that player would then be queued behind one Redis hiccup.
//
// So the order in a transaction that touches both is guild, then player, on
// every process. That is also what keeps two processes joining two players to
// two guilds from deadlocking.
//
//roost:entity id=5 entityKind=EntityKindGuild category=entity.EntityCategoryRemote remote=managed
type Guild struct {
	*entity.RemoteEntityBase
	entity.ComponentManager
	entity.DaoManager
	roster *RosterComponent `comp:"CompTypeRoster"`
	dao    *db.GuildDao     `dao:"guild"`
}
